Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a great set of things to prepare before your annual. A few quick clarifying questions first so I tailor the suggestions correctly:
- Do you currently have a diagnosis of type 2 diabetes, or are you mostly asking about diabetes screening and prevention/risk?  
- Can you confirm your current medication list and what each med is for? (You listed levothyroxine 75 mcg and gabapentin 300 mg but paired them with different conditions — levothyroxine is for thyroid replacement; gabapentin is usually for nerve/pain.) Also: are you taking an anticoagulant for atrial fibrillation?  
- When you say “last creatinine was 72%,” do you mean an eGFR of 72 mL/min/1.73 m2 (i.e., 72), or something else?

While you confirm, here are focused questions, tests, and talking points you can use at your visit with Dr. Amy Foster — split into diabetes-related items and orthopedics/referral items.

Type 2 diabetes — questions to ask and tests to request
- Clarify status and goals
  - “Do I have diabetes or prediabetes based on my recent labs? What should my A1c target be given my age and heart history?”
  - “Given my atrial fibrillation and cardiac care, are there specific glucose targets or meds you prefer for me?”
- Tests to request (if not already done recently)
  - Hemoglobin A1c (or fasting glucose if needed)
  - Basic metabolic panel (BMP) including creatinine and eGFR
  - Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (kidney damage screening)
  - Lipid panel
  - ECG if it hasn’t been done recently or if you have palpitations/changes
- Medication and safety review
  - “Can we review my current meds for interactions with diabetes medicines (including thyroid med and any anticoagulant)?”
  - If you have diabetes, ask whether an SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 receptor agonist would benefit you given cardiovascular/renal risk — and whether your kidney function (eGFR) is adequate for those drugs.
  - If you’re on metformin or considering it: confirm eGFR thresholds and whether it’s still appropriate.
  - Ask about hypoglycemia risk and how to recognize/manage it.
- Preventive care and monitoring
  - “Do I need an annual retinal eye exam, foot exam, or referrals (diabetes educator/nutrition)?”
  - Vaccinations (flu, pneumococcal, shingles) and lifestyle counseling (weight, diet, exercise).
- Practical items to bring/prepare
  - Bring a list of current meds + dosing, recent blood sugar readings if you check them at home, and any recent labs.
- Suggested short script for the visit
  - “I’d like to review screening and management for diabetes today — please check A1c, BMP (or eGFR), and urine albumin. Based on results, can we discuss whether I should start or change any diabetes medicines, especially ones that improve heart and kidney outcomes?”

Orthopedics / referral to Northwest Orthopedics — when to ask for one
- Reasons a PCP typically refers to orthopedics
  - Persistent, function-limiting back pain despite appropriate conservative care (physical therapy, medications, activity modification) for ~6–12 weeks
  - Progressive neurologic deficits (leg weakness, worsening numbness, loss of reflexes)
  - New red flags: bowel/bladder dysfunction, unintentional rapid weight loss, fever, history of cancer, or trauma
  - Suspected structural condition that may need surgery (spinal stenosis, severe herniated disc, unstable spine)
  - Need for specialist assessment if you’re considering advanced interventions (injections, surgery)
- What to have ready / what to ask
  - “My back pain has been [describe frequency/severity], and it limits these activities: [list]. I’ve tried [PT, meds, injections] for [duration]. Given my age and health history, do you recommend a referral to Northwest Orthopedics?”
  - Ask whether to get imaging first (plain X-rays vs MRI). Often PCPs will order X-rays or MRI before referral, but bringing recent imaging is helpful.
  - If you have neurological symptoms, request an expedited referral.
- Conservative-treatment threshold
  - If you’ve had structured physical therapy and appropriate non-op treatments for several weeks without meaningful improvement, it’s reasonable to ask for an orthopedic opinion.
- Suggested short script for the visit
  - “My back pain is [how long/severity]. I’ve done [PT, meds] for [X weeks/months] with [little/no] improvement. Can you refer me to Northwest Orthopedics for specialist assessment and let me know if imaging is needed first?”

Other visit items to coordinate with your PCP (especially relevant given AF and other meds)
- Medication reconciliation: confirm levothyroxine dose, why gabapentin is prescribed, and whether you’re on anticoagulation for AF (this affects procedural decisions).
- Kidney function and med safety: confirm exact creatinine/eGFR numbers—these affect diabetes drug choices and some imaging contrasts or procedures.
- Cardiac precautions: because you have AF, mention cardiology follow-up and whether any orthopedic procedures would need coordination with your cardiologist (anticoagulation management).
- Preventive screens: colon cancer screening status, bone health (DEXA) if concerned about osteoporosis given age.

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short, one-paragraph message you can bring or send to Dr. Foster that covers both diabetes screening/management and a request for an orthopedics referral (and I can tailor the wording once you confirm whether you have diabetes and your exact meds and creatinine/eGFR).
- Or create a printable checklist you can take to the appointment.

Tell me: do you want a short draft message to send to Dr. Foster now? And please confirm the medication list and what you meant by “72%” for creatinine so I can be precise.

